# Runtime artefacts this kit's own hooks write into the repository they gate.
#
#   .claude/.verified      the verification stamp
#   .claude/.failed-runs   the flake ledger's record of failures
#   .claude/.test-change   declarations authorising a test edit, 30-minute TTL
#   .claude/.flaky         a pass recorded as flaky rather than clean
#   .claude/.edit-count    edits seen since the last stamp
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is not tidiness.
#
# COMMITTING .verified HANDS THE NEXT CLONE A GATE THAT IS ALREADY OPEN.
# verify-gate.sh reads the stamp's age from its MTIME — `stat -c %Y`, not the
# timestamp written inside the file — and git sets mtime to checkout time. So a
# committed stamp does not arrive stale and refused, which is what it looks like
# it would do. It arrives SECONDS OLD. The first commit anyone makes after cloning
# is allowed without running anything, attesting to a suite that last passed on a
# different machine, possibly months earlier.
#
# That is this kit's own worst failure shape — a gate reporting a pass it did not
# earn, failing in the reassuring direction — shipped in the repository that exists
# to document it. Until 2026-09-17 nothing stopped a `git add -A` from doing it.
#
# A GLOB, NOT THE EXPLICIT LIST, and that is the opposite of controls.list on
# purpose. That list names suites which must EXIST, where a glob cannot tell you
# one has gone missing. This excludes files which must NOT be committed, where the
# failure mode is a NEW artefact nobody remembered to add to the list. The glob is
# the fail-closed direction here and an explicit list is not. The names above are a
# comment for the reader, not the mechanism.
#
# Scoped to dotfiles so a future .claude/agents/ or .claude/settings.json stays
# committable — check-models.sh validates agent definitions in a commit, so that
# directory is expected to be tracked one day.
.claude/.*
